#
# (kuna divergence, DIV-78) The libstdc++ std::__throw_* exception-throw helpers
# (<bits/functexcept.h>, plus __throw_regex_error from <bits/regex_error.h>).
# Every one is declared __attribute__((__noreturn__)) upstream and every body ends
# in _GLIBCXX_THROW_OR_ABORT -- a throw, or abort() when built -fno-exceptions -- so
# none of them can return. Upstream Ghidra's list omits the whole family, so on any
# C++ binary that uses std::string / std::vector / std::function the code after such
# a call is emitted as if it were reachable (GH-273).
#
# Two spellings per helper, because two different matchers consume this list:
#   * the mangled .dynsym/.symtab form, which the analysis-tier scan
#     (noreturn_known) matches BEFORE demangling. Written as a trailing-`*` wildcard
#     so a signature change cannot age the entry out -- these helpers do gain
#     overloads (__throw_ios_failure gained a `PKci` form in GCC 7). The
#     `ZSt<len>__throw_<name>` prefix is an exact Itanium-ABI encoding of
#     `std::__throw_<name>`, so the wildcard cannot reach any other symbol.
#   * the demangled base name, which is what the flow-time name matcher
#     (noreturn_externmatch) sees; `std::__throw_length_error` reduces to
#     `throw_length_error` under the namespace guard + leading-underscore strip.
